@Anonymous

"Imagine all the folks who complain about the puzzles being too easy are avid bowlers, most of whom have been competing in a league for years/decades"

If you've been competing at something for that long you're going to find it easier than you did before, bumpers or no bumpers.

@Anonymous

Pretty good analogy except that expert bowlers probably never get gutter balls anyway. So let’s say they made the pins less balanced so they fall more easily, or shorten the alley. That’s also a better analogy because the newer bowlers think they are doing terrific and feeling proud with their high scores and their long games streaks with an over 200 score, but don’t know what they are missing.

Either way, the additional point is, it’s not like there aren’t some easier lanes already for new players to learn to practice on (M-W). And the bowling alley may think they are making more money by attracting new bowlers. But really, those bowlers are gong to get better and then they too will eventually get bored. Or, the expert bowlers will eventually leave by attrition and the new bowlers will never get as good because they never get to hone their skills to the same degree, which is bad for the sport of bowling. And what that means is, even if another alley tries to open with more difficult lanes, it will struggle because there won’t be anyone to appreciate them.

@SP

Funny, both aspects of the analogy that you pointed out occurred to me a bit later: experienced bowlers probably wouldn’t benefit too much from bumpers, and the analogized NYT crossword bowling alley did already have bumpers, just not *every* day.

Regardless, I like the simplicity of the original metaphor — both because the obviousness of the before-and-after matches the starkness of difference in difficulty between pre- and post-Easening late-week puzzles, and because I think a lot of people can easily see how it would be crazy to insist on keeping bumpers on *all* bowling lanes to accommodate newer bowlers.

@David
This may have changed in the past week, but we have found AI very bad at adding any more than two numbers at a time.

It can interpret imprecise speech well, but can't predict the outcome of adding a stack of numbers. So I wouldn't trust its prediction for the results of t-tests (unless this has changed in the past week). It can write code, however, so you could probably ask it to write a program that could add the column for you. Then ask it to debug the code when it added incorrectly.

